    "created_at": "2024-07-11T16:27:52Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-07-11T16:27:52Z",
    "body": "The exhaustive list is: HTML uses a different parser that does not correctly parse SGML, and SGML parsers cannot correctly parse valid HTML. The language has *never* actually been valid SGML in common usage, and browsers diverged into custom parsers very quickly, back in HTML 2/3 days 30+ years ago. HTML5 just finally codified this with a real parser, rather than letting people figure out all the divergences they had to implement themselves. (If you limit yourself only to *valid* HTML, it's much less problematic, but the fact that invalid HTML exists and still needs to be parsed consistently is the main reason Hixie had to write the HTML5 parser.)\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, I highly doubt that listing incompatibilities with more precision will be considered worth the time of anyone with interest in editting, and I also doubt it would be considered worthwhile to include in the spec in any case, as there is ~0 usage of SGML (documents or parsers) in the wild these days anyway. We don't even have such a list for divergences from XML, which remains a relevant language.",
